The change to `backchannel_provider_for` is a pure refactor: it replaces `getattr(providers.first(), ...)` (which raised `AttributeError` on `None`) with an explicit `None` check returning `None`. This is a bug fix, not an authorization change. The callers (`configuration.py`, `jwks.py`) already handle the `None` case with `if not provider: raise Http404`, and their authorization model (public SSF discovery endpoints with `AllowAny` / plain `View`) is unchanged by this PR. No new authorization exposure is introduced by the changed lines.

